        "answer": "CONCEPT AND RESPONSIBILITY\n\nRebase replays commits onto another base and rewrites commit history.\n\nCATEGORY-SPECIFIC EXPLANATION\n\nExplain the definition first, then the responsibility, major components and a simple operational example.\n\nPRACTICAL VERIFICATION\n\nUse the following command or operational interface:\n\ngit rebase origin/main\n\nInterpret the result in the context of the affected resource. Confirm the resource identity, environment, configuration and current operational state before making changes.\n\nPRODUCTION FAILURE EXAMPLE\n\nRebase stops with conflicts\n\nROOT-CAUSE INVESTIGATION\n\nResolve each conflict, run git add, continue with git rebase --continue or safely abort.\n\nOPERATIONAL RESPONSE\n\n1. Identify the affected application, resource, environment and users.\n2. Check service or resource status and recent configuration changes.\n3. Inspect relevant application logs, platform events and monitoring metrics.\n4. Verify network connectivity, dependencies, permissions and resource availability.\n5. Compare the current state with the last known healthy state.\n6. Apply the smallest approved corrective change.\n7. Verify application functionality and monitor for recurring failures.\n8. Record the root cause, corrective action and preventive measure.\n\nINTERVIEW EXPECTATION\n\nA complete answer should explain why the component is needed, how it works, how an engineer verifies it, what can fail and how the engineer restores service safely. Avoid making changes blindly or exposing secrets in logs and terminal output."
